weave-knee trial v2
fanout: 6
seed: 1
weaver: claude-sonnet-5
judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 4
narrative:
  Kelnex is a six-node ring, and the ring is the whole story: Ostkel hands its sealed ledger to Mirtol, Mirtol streams its evictions to Nexmir, Nexmir delegates overflow to Ostnex, Ostnex signals backpressure to Pamost, Pamost reports missed heartbeats to Grisax, and Grisax closes the circuit by publishing its sweep verdicts back to Ostkel.

  Reliability degrades as the ledger moves off the vault: Ostkel will retry a failed handoff up to 3 times before parking it, but the ledger it hands to Mirtol lands at a cache that retries only once before parking — the sealed ledger has far less room to fail once it leaves Ostkel than it had inside it. Further round the ring, Grisax restores some slack, retrying a failed handoff up to 2 times before parking; so the ring's three stated retry budgets (3, 1, 2) sit at three of its six stations, with Mirtol as the fragile point between them.

  Payload handling alternates around the ring in lockstep with position: Ostkel, Nexmir, and Pamost each keep only digests of what they carry, never bodies — every second station along the Ostkel→Mirtol→Nexmir→Ostnex→Pamost→Grisax path is a digest-only station, so no full body survives more than one hop before being reduced to a fingerprint.

  Reading behavior clusters the other way: Mirtol, Ostnex, Pamost, and Grisax all answer reads from their newest snapshot only — four of the six modules, essentially the back three-quarters of the ring from the cache onward, so once data leaves Ostkel's vault it is read only ever as of its latest snapshot, never historically, all the way to the relay.

  Two modules gate action on consensus: Ostkel acts only once 2 of its peers countersign, and so does Ostnex — the vault and the scheduler, sitting on opposite sides of the ring, are its two countersigned choke points, while the other four act unilaterally.

  Eviction windows vary station to station: Nexmir and Grisax both evict anything left unclaimed after 5 ticks — the ring's tightest, shared floor — while Ostnex holds unclaimed items longest, for 12 ticks, and Pamost sits between them at 7. So the same overflow that Nexmir sheds at 5 ticks can, once delegated to Ostnex, linger more than twice as long before eviction there.

  Finally, Mirtol and Nexmir, adjacent on the ring, each refuse new writes while their own sweep is running — the two write-blocking stations sit back to back, so a sweep at the cache and a sweep at the monitor are the two places incoming traffic can stall consecutively before ever reaching the scheduler, ledger, or relay beyond them.
